cbmconvert 0.9.1 - convert different Commodore binary archives to LHARC format

Traditional archiving systems for the Commodore 64 do not compress the
files at all, or they have poor compression rate. To minimize
transmission times and space needed for Commodore binaries on file
retrieval systems, you need a better archiving format.

The LHARC archiver has been ported to the Commodore 128 years ago in
the CS-DOS system, but you need a Commodore 128 with a RAM Expansion
Unit to use it. As they are difficult to obtain nowadays, and as the
program would run very slowly on the C-128, being able to use LHARC on
a bigger system is a great relief. Extracting files from LHARC
archives is possible also with a stock C-64.

The cbmconvert package allows you to convert different CBM file
archives to LHARC format. It is most useful on file retrieval systems
storing Commodore files. Re-archiving old files may halve the file
lengths or cut them even more. You can also use it yourself when you
want to distribute your binaries in tightest possible format. Just
pack the files on your C-64 with your favourite program, and convert
the archive on a Unix system before uploading to the net.

Currently cbmconvert accepts files created with Arkive, Lynx and
ZipCode. The software to extract ARChived files and self-extracting
archives (SDA) exists, but it is not included to this version because
of copyright reasons. The module for ARC will hopefully be included to
the next version of this package.



SOFTWARE REQUIREMENTS

- Unix compatible operating system with standard tools

- LHARC for Unix (lha-1.00.tar.Z or similar)
	If the "lha" command is not recognized on your system,
	you have to compile this piece of software as well.

- SFX header (sfx.ml) from an SFX file
	This file is missing because of copyright reasons.
	You can get it from any new SFX file by issuing the
	command "head -3721c file.sfx > sfx.ml". Older SFX
	files may have an SFX header of different length.



HOW TO USE CBMCONVERT

First install cbmconvert to your binaries subdirectory. Create sfx.ml
as described above, and edit the Makefile to reflect your system
settings. Then "make install".

After the installation step, you have two scripts to use. For all
other archives except ZipCoded files, use "convert". The script
"zipconvert" takes care of the ZipCoded archives.

The "convert" script works on following file formats:

	Format               	File suffix
	======		     	===========
	Arkive		   	.ark
	Lynx			.lnx

For instance, to convert a Lynxed file "software.lnx" to LHARC format,
use "convert software.lnx". If you don't get any error messages, it
should be safe to delete the original file, "rm software.lnx". You can
specify multiple filenames on the command line, e.g.
"convert *.lnx *.ark".

The conversion may lose some information. Filenames will be converted
from PETSCII to ASCII, and some PETSCII specific characters will be
distorted when converting back from ASCII to PETSCII before
extraction.  Arkive stores some non-standard directory information,
which will be ignored and left out of the LHARC files.

Both Lynx and Arkive allow duplicate filenames. Only the first of them
is extracted. These duplicates are often used as directory separators,
to separate groups of filenames. If you really need such separators,
use 1-block-long PRG or SEQ files with different names. For example,
the first separator can be a string of 16 dashes, the second one can
be 15 dashes, and so on.

Arkive also works on illegal filetypes that cannot be created without
directly modifying the directory blocks. As the LHARC format is
device-independent, these types must be ignored. In this case you will
be warned. And Arkive handles relative files in a very stupid way.
If you have an ARK file with one or more relative files, you should check
that the files are extracted correctly.

ZipCode is the most 1541-dependent format this package can work on. It
is probably best to leave these archives alone. However, many people
use to pack regular 1541 disks with ZipCode. The "zipconvert" script
is meant for this type of ZipCoded archives. You will be warned about
any non-standard things in the disk image. The ZipCoded archives
consist of four files prefixed with a number from 1 to 4, and a
exclamation point. To extract an archive having the files "1!disk",
"2!disk", "3!disk" and "4!disk", command "zipconvert disk".  You may
then delete the original files with "rm ?\!disk" or similar. Like
convert, zipconvert accepts multiple filenames on the command line.

Support for ZipCode SixPacks ("1!!disk", "2!!disk", ..., "6!!disk")
could be added easily, but this format is used mainly for really
custom 1541 disks with abnormal low-level format.

If the resulting LHARC file is less than 47479 bytes long, it will be
prefixed with the self-extraction header so that you can extract it on
a C-64 (or C-128). Otherwise it will be left without that header, and
you can extract it only on a C-128 running CS-DOS and LHX. In the
future there will probably be a LHX for C-64 as well. LZH files do not
have any size limits except the 1541 disk size, 664 blocks, which is
168656 bytes. The SFX header has other drawbacks as well: it can only
extract PRG and SEQ files.



TECHNICAL DETAILS

The two shell scripts "convert" and "zipconvert" control following
binary programs:

	Name			Function
	====			========
	unark			Extract files from Arkives.
	unlynx			Extract files from Lynx archives.
	zip2disk		Extract a disk image from ZipCoded files.
	disk2files		Extract files from a disk image.
	lha			Create LHARC files.
	lzhconvert		Rename files in LHArchives.

Temporary files will be created in the "tmp" subdirectory of your home
directory.



Filename translation

To prevent any distortion in the filenames, the files will be
extracted under names like "FILEnnnnn", where "nnnnn" is an
integer. Names for these temporary files are created by scan.c. The
filename translation table will be redirected to a file "archive.list"
where "archive" is replaced with the archive name to be converted.

The temporary filenames and their original names are listed in a file
called listfile. Blank lines and lines starting with a semicolon are
ignored. The files are listed as follows:

	"oldname1"	"newname1"
	"oldname2"	"newname2"
	...

You may also use spaces or commas as filename separators. All current
cbmconvert modules separate the filenames with horizontal tabulators.
To enter special characters, you have to escape them like in C. For
instance, to enter a filename "file\name" with the quotes, use
"\"file\\name\"". 

The following escape sequences are supported:

	\a    alert           (BEL, \7,   \x7)
	\b    backspace       (BS,  \10,  \x8)
	\f    form feed       (FF,  \14,  \xc)
	\n    new line        (LF,  \12,  \xa)
	\r    return          (CR,  \15,  \xd)
	\t    tabulator       (HT,  \11,  \x9)
	\v    vertical tab    (VT,  \13,  \xb)
	\"    quotation mark  (",   \42,  \x22)
	\'    apostrophe      (',   \47,  \x27)
	\\    backslash       (\,   \134, \x5c)
	\?    question mark   (?,   \77,  \x3f)
	\nnn  octal number         (max. 3 digits)
	\xnn  hexadecimal number   (max. 2 digits)

The cbmconvert modules use the octal notation to specify all other special
characters except \ and ", which are prefixed with \.

The CS-DOS's LHARC format has to record the file type somehow. It
suffixes the file names with a null byte and a letter indicating file
type. Relative files' record length will be stored after the file type
indicator. Here's an example of a listfile that you would use to
create files with different CBM file types:

	; host filename			Commodore filename
	"PROGRAM.PRG"			"program\0P"
	"SEQUENTIAL.SEQ"		"sequential file\0S"
	"USER.USR"			"user file\0U"
	; relative file with a record length of 123 bytes (173 octal)
	"RELATIVE.REL"			"relative file\0R\173"

Note that LHARC for MS-DOS and lha for Unix write the filenames in
upper case, when they are in generic or old-style mode.

By default, CS-DOS LHX and the SFX header create PRG files. This is
why cbmconvert modules leave the "\0P" suffix out.

Note: The self-extraction header extracts only PRG and SEQ files;
it does not create REL and USR files properly.


How lzhconvert works

The "lzhconvert" module has multiple functions. If you command
"lzhconvert archive.lzh", it will print a list of the files in
the archive in the listfile format. The right column contains
default temporary file names. This feature could be used to
convert SFX and LHARC files to some other format. To see the
archive contents, use "lzhconvert archive.lzh | cut -f1".

If you specify two filenames, lzhconvert will read the first
one and write the converted file under the second name. It will
ignore any self-extraction header and padding in the input file.
Lzhconvert will read the listfile from standard input and then
start converting. In addition to the filename conversion, it
converts the archive from header level 1 to header level 0,
leaving pathnames and other additional information out. Note
that Unix-lha uses header level 2 by default, which is not
supported by lzhconvert.

Typical usage of lzhconvert is first making the listfile
(lzhconvert archive.lzh > listfile), then editing it, and
finally converting the archive
(lzhconvert archive.lzh archive.tmp < listfile).



IN CASE OF ERROR

If you get any error message from "convert" or "zipconvert", you
should examine at least the temporary list file ($HOME/tmp/*.list).
It may contain some comments explaining what is wrong in some files.
You may want to construct the LZH archive manually from the files
in that directory.



